  "answer": "To reduce the risk of users over-generalizing from partial information, it has been proposed to deploy coverage-augmented algorithmic teaching of robot objectives to end-users (https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.03465). Before real operation, users are shown a purposefully curated set of robot behaviors that teach the robot's objective by maximizing the Euclidean-model posterior on the true reward parameters and covering every trajectory-strategy cluster (https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.03465). The mechanism relies on coverage augmentation after posterior convergence: once the additional posterior gain from teaching examples falls below a threshold (ε), the system adds extra examples by selecting one per uncovered strategy cluster (https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.03465). By ensuring users see at least one example from each major trajectory-strategy cluster, this approach prevents them from over-generalizing and quickly aligns their mental models with the robot's true reward function, allowing them to better anticipate the autonomous car's future actions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.03465)."
